# Project: Task Management API

## Code Style
- Use ES6+ syntax (const/let, arrow functions, async/await)
- Use strict equality (===)
- Always use try-catch for async operations
- Add JSDoc comments to exported functions and classes

## Security
- NEVER hardcode secrets - use environment variables
- Validate ALL user inputs before processing
- Use bcrypt for password hashing with salt rounds >= 10
- Use parameterized queries to prevent injection attacks
- Sanitize user input before displaying

## Naming Conventions
- Functions/Variables: camelCase (getUserById, taskList)
- Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (MAX_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS, JWT_EXPIRY)
- Files: kebab-case (auth-middleware.js, user-service.js)
- Classes: PascalCase (UserModel, TaskController)

## Error Handling
- Return consistent JSON format: { success: false, message: string, error?: object }
- Never swallow errors silently - always log with context
- Use proper HTTP status codes (400, 401, 404, 500, etc.)
- Include request IDs in error logs for tracing

## Testing
- Write tests for all new features
- Test both success and error cases
- Use descriptive test names: "should return 404 when task not found"
- Maintain minimum 80% code coverage
- Use Jest and supertest for API testing

## API Design
- Use RESTful conventions for endpoints
- Version APIs if needed (/api/v1/...)
- Return consistent response formats
- Include proper CORS headers
- Document all endpoints

## Performance
- Avoid N+1 queries
- Use pagination for list endpoints
- Implement rate limiting in production
- Cache frequently accessed data
- Use async/await properly to avoid blocking

## Documentation
- Add JSDoc comments for public APIs
- Keep README.md updated with API endpoints
- Document environment variables in .env.example
- Include usage examples for complex functions

## Git Commits
- Use conventional commit format: type(scope): message
- Types: feat, fix, docs, refactor, test, chore
- Keep commits focused and atomic
- Write descriptive commit messages
